sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    actor Dev as PR Author
    participant CL as GitHub PR / VS Code panel
    participant GW as API Gateway
    participant GH as GitHub API
    participant DB as PostgreSQL

    Note over Dev,CL: precondition — the review produced at least one<br/>suggestion whose confidence cleared the threshold

    Dev->>CL: open the suggested-change comment or finding card
    CL-->>Dev: show original snippet vs suggested snippet

    alt developer clicks Commit suggestion (GitHub)
        Dev->>CL: Commit suggestion
        CL->>GW: POST /v1/findings/{id}/suggestion/accept (target = github_branch)
        GW->>DB: check suggestion state is proposed
        alt already resolved or superseded by a newer commit
            DB-->>GW: state = accepted / rejected / superseded
            GW-->>CL: 409 conflict
            CL-->>Dev: this suggestion is no longer current
        else still open
            GW->>GH: commit the suggestion to the PR branch
            GH-->>GW: new commit sha
            GW->>DB: state = accepted, resolved_by_user_id, commit sha
            GW-->>CL: 200 { state: accepted, commitSha }
            CL-->>Dev: change applied to the branch
        end

    else developer clicks Apply fix (VS Code)
        Dev->>CL: Apply fix
        CL->>CL: write the snippet into the open editor buffer
        CL->>GW: POST /v1/findings/{id}/suggestion/accept (target = editor_buffer)
        GW->>DB: state = accepted, resolved_by_user_id
        GW-->>CL: 200 recorded
        CL-->>Dev: buffer updated, nothing pushed to GitHub

    else developer rejects, or does nothing
        Dev->>CL: Reject (optionally with a reason)
        CL->>GW: POST /v1/findings/{id}/suggestion/reject
        GW->>DB: state = rejected, reason
        GW-->>CL: 200 recorded
        CL-->>Dev: finding stays visible, no code changed
    end

    Note over GW,DB: CodeSentinel never writes to a branch on its own.<br/>Acceptance is the only path to a commit (FR-GH-04, AC-04).

    Note over DB: on the next review of this file the finding is<br/>matched by dedupe_key and reported as resolved
